WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:05.730
There's a wealth of hidden treasures in Mamma
Mia 2 for fans to enjoy, with little connections,

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:10.230
nods and winks to costumes, choreography,
music, jokes, and more from the first film.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:14.980
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video I'm going to reveal 10 amazing

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:20.170
details you may have missed in the Mamma Mia
sequel which will make you love it even more!

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:24.039
The special connection between Donna and Sophie,
and the parallels between the two characters,

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:25.770
are at the heart of Here We Go Again.

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.990
For example, there's a delightful link back
to young Donna's silver butterfly necklace

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:34.130
in the christening scene where Sophie wears
a dress with a butterfly pattern.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:38.540
The film's costume designer chose that particular
design as the idea was that Sophie was wearing

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.950
the dress in memory of her mother.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.150
The horseshoe necklace that Sophie wears in
the sequel is also a sweet and subtle link

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:49.620
back to her mother and how her experience
on the island changed her life.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.280
The first night young Donna stayed in the
old farmhouse on the hill, there was a big

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.370
storm which frightened a horse in the stables.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:00.460
Taking care of that scared horse not only
brought a young Sam into Donna's life, but

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.710
also ultimately led her to open the hotel.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:07.280
That's because Sofia, who owned the building,
believed you could "judge a person's heart

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.119
by the way they treat animals", and the fact
that Donna was so kind to Sofia's horse Hector

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:16.759
led her to offer the old farmhouse to Donna
to live in for free if she did it up.

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:20.810
Remember Bill and Sophie's conversation in
the first film after he realised he might

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:21.810
be her father.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.119
"You know what comes next?"

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:26.690
"You're not going to tell me you have a twin
sister, are you?"

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:31.500
Well, Here We Go Again gives an amusing explanation
for Bill's reaction in the original film.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:36.280
As we find out in the sequel, Bill himself
has a twin brother, who pretends to be Bill

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:41.369
at a prestigious award ceremony so the real
Bill can go to the opening party for Sophie's

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:43.350
hotel on the same night.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.249
When young Donna gets chased by a goat during
The Name of the Game scene, it feels like

00:01:47.249 --> 00:01:51.579
a little nod back to the first film when Meryl
Streep's Donna was surprised by a goat while

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:53.390
singing Mamma Mia!

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:57.579
In the original film, Sam, Harry, and Bill
stayed in the old goathouse when Sophie tried

00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:00.310
to hide them from her mother when they first
arrived on the island.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:05.189
And I also wonder whether the "really amusing
story" about a goat that Bill's twin brother

00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:09.629
wanted to tell at the awards ceremony had
anything to do with the goats at Sofia's old

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:10.879
farmhouse.

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:13.900
Remember that Sofia was actually Bill and
his brother's great-aunt.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.430
"Well, I'm named after a Sofia."

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:20.590
"I used to have a great-aunt living on the
mainland named Sofia."

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:25.170
A crucial piece of Donna's wardrobe in the
first movie was her denim dungarees, and the

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:26.540
sequel shows their origin.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:31.140
But even before young Donna purchases her
signature outfit in the new film, the costume

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.400
department cleverly lays the trail for that
moment, dressing Lily James in denim shorts

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.860
and bikini, blue tops, denim jackets, and,
later in the film, denim flares.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:44.360
And the Dynamos' "When I Kissed The Teacher"
outfits also have an interesting origin.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.520
If you look really closely in Donna's student
room in Oxford, you'll notice there's some

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:52.500
material missing from the brown, yellow, and
cream-striped curtains.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.920
The idea was to hint at the inventiveness
of Donna, Tanya, and Rosie who made their

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.810
outfits themselves out of those very curtains.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:04.370
The concept for the Dynamos' cut-out curtain
clothes was inspired by movies such as Gone

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:07.310
With The Wind, The Sound Of Music, and Enchanted.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:12.310
And costume designer Michele Clapton added
"stuck-on stars and fake-fur boas" to enhance

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:13.829
the 70s vibe.

00:03:13.829 --> 00:03:17.720
Here We Go Again features some really fun
call-backs to the choreography in the first

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:18.720
film.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.260
For example, at the very end of the dance
routine for "When I Kissed The Teacher", the

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:27.290
frame freezes on young Donna as she's about
to dive-bomb into the river, just as we saw

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:31.270
Meryl Streep's Donna do at the end of "Dancing
Queen" in the first film, though Streep did

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:32.620
actually jump in!

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.730
According to writer-director Ol Parker, the
reason we don't get to see the Dynamos jump

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:40.170
into the water is that it would have held
up filming as the actresses would have got

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:44.280
soaked and needed lots of time to fix their
hair and clothes again for each take.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.060
So, instead, they jumped onto a crash pad
that's out of shot!

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.780
And there's another cool call-back during
the dance routine for "Why Did It Have To

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:52.780
Be Me?"

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.049
when young Donna pinches Bill's hat and pops
it on her own head, just like Meryl Streep's

00:03:57.049 --> 00:04:00.970
Donna did to the ship's captain during her
performance of Money Money Money in the original

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:01.970
film.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:06.311
It was really beautiful how Meryl Streep and
Amanda Seyfried's choreography during "My

00:04:06.311 --> 00:04:09.810
Love, My Life" mirrored their performance
during "Slipping Through My Fingers" in the

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.060
first film.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:14.780
Each scene takes place at a very emotional
point in the story.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:18.670
Donna sings Slipping Through My Fingers as
she helps Sophie get ready for her wedding,

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:23.150
and the way they stand together here coincides
with the song's lyrics, 'Sometimes I wish

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:26.729
that I could freeze the picture and save it
from the funny tricks of time.'

00:04:26.729 --> 00:04:30.470
Mamma Mia 2 calls back to that image of them
in front of the mirror when Donna makes her

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:34.990
first appearance reflected in the chapel's
baptismal font, and as they join together

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:40.389
singing My Love, My Life, the lyrics 'Like
reflections of your mind' feel especially

00:04:40.389 --> 00:04:41.699
fitting.

00:04:41.699 --> 00:04:45.910
There's a lovely bit of musical symmetry between
the sequel and the first film.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:50.360
In the original movie, after the curtain call
performance of Waterloo, Amanda Seyfried's

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:53.840
version of "Thank You For The Music" plays
over the closing credits.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:58.080
And the sequel mirrors this as Seyfried again
sings Thank You For The Music, this time as

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.140
Sophie writes invitations for the hotel's
opening party.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:05.680
And there's another delightful musical connection
between the beginning of each film.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.680
In the sequel, when Sophie tells Cienfuegos
that she needs him to make sure the place

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.360
is ready for the party rather than post the
invites, he replies, 'Whatever you dream,

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.300
Miss Sophie, we'll make real.'

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:20.770
Which is a nice link back to the original
movie which began and ended with Sophie singing

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:25.759
"I Have A Dream", first as she posted letters
to her three potential fathers and later as

00:05:25.759 --> 00:05:30.070
she left her dads and mum behind on the island
to travel with her boyfriend Sky.

